#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172885} :activity/id #uuid "81dd1d17-811a-52af-9ad3-b3247253617b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050760 :topic/id #uuid "64670a5e-dd35-51fc-bd24-210590f74fcd" :topic/math-academy-id 3336} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172885 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173152 :tutorial/content "The general equation of a second order (quadratic) curve is an expression of the form \n\n$$\n\\alpha_{11}x_1^2+2\\alpha_{12}x_1x_2 + \\alpha_{22}x_2^2 + 2\\alpha_{13}x_1+ 2\\alpha_{23}x_2 +\\alpha_{33}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere  $x_1$ and $x_2$ are variables, and $\\alpha_{ij} \\in \\Bbb R$ for all $1\\leq i,j \\leq 3.$ \n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n3x_1^2-x_1x_2-5x_2^2+5x_2 - 1 = 0\n$$\n\nis a general quadratic curve." :tutorial/id #uuid "2d9b3527-1999-5169-affa-96a37f99fb01" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5004 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8cf7cde9-4168-5bb5-a77b-f1c02aa56436" :step/math-academy-id 31225 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172919}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172886 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172028 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171834 :question/id #uuid "1ecd84f8-5157-595a-93b6-a865acaa53d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8378"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26871e46-51ca-5680-a064-bcee4ac984fd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048300}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572671}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type"} :step/id #uuid "b2f2a127-b40d-5b8f-ba64-4a1cad0e08c9" :step/math-academy-id 31226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172887 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173153 :tutorial/content "In general, the cross-term of a quadratic equation can be eliminated by an orthogonal change of variables. That is, one of the kind \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y} ,\n$$\n\n  where the columns of $P$ form an orthonormal  eigenbasis for the corresponding quadratic form's matrix.\n\nLet $\\mathcal C$ be a plane curve defined as \n\n$$\n\\mathcal C:\\: 2x_1x_2 -2\\sqrt2x_1+2\\sqrt2x_2 =8.\n$$\n\nHow do we determine the type of our curve?\n\nNotice that the quadratic form $2x_1x_2$ can be reduced to canonical form $y_1^2-y_2^2$ using the change of variables\n![](images/ab/ab1ecd406bcaf2f48f60d0e97c214f1fae9ab911b08ebe2bd74a6e43e0aa524b.svg)\n\nFirst, let's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n![](images/18/1820ce650da02daa432023803a773821f63753e2adda1c6e55964fdddb913934.svg)\n\nWe know that this change of variables reduces the corresponding quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) = 2x_1x_2$ to its canonical form. Indeed, performing the change of variables on $\\mathcal C,$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}=8\\,⟹\\,{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+4{y}_{2}=8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n and, by completing the  square for the variable $y_2$,  we get the equation \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-2)}^{2} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the resulting equation in the $y_1,y_2$ variables is the following.\n\n![](images/d5/d532f5207a9d518f92024c86689285834b86c961d0f647d6ee99ad6dd9c7c329.png)\n\nNotice that we can write down the change-of-variables matrix as\n![](images/2d/2d128752c7b7a73db6a3dfc8c1fc232347b01d50376b46b08704a9ebd4e3ceb7.svg)\nwhich is a rotation matrix. Looking at the diagram above, we see that going from one coordinate system to another can be viewed as a rotation of our axes by $45^\\circ.$\n\n**Watch out!** In general, this action is an orthogonal transformation (it can be a rotation, a reflection, or a combination of such transformations).\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-2)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis (standard) equation represents a hyperbola in which  both semiaxes are equal to $2.$      \n\nIn the diagram below we represent the curve in the $z_1,z_2$ variables.\n\n![](images/c3/c36709f752bd9c319662e7e2150c54031cc8fb3934be37711c65e7a7668829ca.png)\n\nLooking at the diagram above, we see that in the final step we simply translated our system by $(0,2)$ in $y$-coordinates.\n\n**Note:** Finding the simplest equation of a quadratic curve can be viewed as the following series of steps:\n\n- **Step 1**. We eliminate the cross-term by reducing the quadratic part to canonical form. Geometrically, this is equivalent to an orthogonal transformation of our coordinate system.\n\n- **Step 2**. We eliminate the linear terms by completing the squares. Geometrically, this is equivalent to a translation of our coordinate system." :tutorial/id #uuid "cd5dde1f-7d5c-58d9-8674-6bf984a51d0d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5005 :tutorial/title "Classifying a General Quadratic Curve"} :step/id #uuid "e347e125-d8fa-58ef-8dc4-81fc67d191f3" :step/math-academy-id 31227 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172886}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172888 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172029 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171835 :question/id #uuid "c03c6fbb-176a-5f30-b928-73908cb93277" :question/math-academy-id "e-8380"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a6bbb34-a43f-5e16-8a24-25c0d6970a38" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049456}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557300}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type From Its Reduced Equation: No Cross-Product Term"} :step/id #uuid "3eb3af03-a01d-5d40-94ed-828350b2cacf" :step/math-academy-id 31229 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172891}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172889 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172030 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171836 :question/id #uuid "58f6a3e7-e867-576a-ad84-0debbbc38d90" :question/math-academy-id "e-8381"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b74a69-2c2c-5286-a987-fee946fd48b6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051078}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557330}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type: No Cross-Product Term"} :step/id #uuid "8393be76-0567-5bf7-bd97-bfad1ef56506" :step/math-academy-id 31230 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172887}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172891 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173155 :tutorial/content "A quadratic equation with no cross-terms can be rewritten in standard form by completing the square. Let's see how this is done in the following example. \n\nSuppose we are given the equation  \n\n$$\n2x_1^2+x_2^2 - 4x_1 + 4x_2 = 2,\n$$\n\nwhat type of curve does it represent?\n\nFirst, we complete the squares for the variables $x_1$ and $x_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1})+({x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}) & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+1-1)+({x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}+4-4) & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}-2+({x}_{2}+2)^{2}-4 & =2 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}+({x}_{2}+2)^{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-1, \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{8})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents an ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{8}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bd1f3565-37df-5db7-aa06-a573b6b58f8a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5015 :tutorial/title "Classifying a Quadratic Curve That Does Not Contain a Cross-Product Term"} :step/id #uuid "ea16c9e4-6358-52d5-9437-e5c11b9cdeec" :step/math-academy-id 31358 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172889}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172919 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173165 :tutorial/content "There are three basic so-called *non-degenerate quadratic curves*:\n\n| Standard Equation | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=1$ | Ellipse |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=1$ | Hyperbola |\n| $x_2^2= 2px_1$ | Parabola |" :tutorial/id #uuid "31519439-ac97-5940-b01b-de36561f9618" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5129 :tutorial/title "Non-Degenerate Quadratic Curves"} :step/id #uuid "1e64c954-a22d-5a07-9a29-b00dae41d7a4" :step/math-academy-id 32209 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172920}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172920 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173166 :tutorial/content "There are also a few so-called *degenerate (real) quadratic curves:*\n\n| Standard Equation | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ | Pair of\n intersecting  lines |\n| ${x_i^2}=a^2$ | Pair of \n parallel lines |\n| ${x_i^2}=0$ | Pair of \n coincident lines |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ | Single point |\n\nLet's see why these equations define the described curves:\n\n- $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ is equivalent to $(bx_1 - ax_2)(bx_1 + ax_2)=0$ which in turn gives equations of two lines $bx_1 - ax_2=0$ and $bx_1 + ax_2=0$ intersecting at the origin.\n\n- ${x_i^2}=a^2$ is equivalent to $(x_i - a)(x_i + a)=0$ which in turn gives equations of two parallel lines $x_i =a$ and $x_i=-a.$\n\n- ${x_i^2}=0$ gives equations of two coincident lines $x_i =0$ and $-x_i=0.$\n\n- $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=0$ is equivalent to $b^2x_1^2+a^2x_2^2=0$ which in turn gives a single point, the origin, since the sum of two squares is $0$ if and only if both squares are $0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7fa5c07c-714f-555e-9253-e8c6b56484e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5130 :tutorial/title "Degenerate (Real) Quadratic Curves"} :step/id #uuid "e4b30b5f-3e46-516c-bdb2-28d2099fba5a" :step/math-academy-id 32210 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172921}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172921 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173167 :tutorial/content "Finally, some quadratic equations might define an *empty set* (or so-called *imaginary curve*):\n\n| Standard Equation | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2}=-1$ | Imaginary ellipse |\n| $x_i^2=-a^2$ | Pair of imaginary\n parallel lines |\n\nLet's see why these equations define the described curves:\n\n- Although there are no real solutions, the equation $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{b^2} = -1,$ which is equivalent to $\\dfrac{x_1^2}{(a\\textrm{i})^2} + \\dfrac{x_2^2}{(b\\textrm{i})^2} = 1,$ has imaginary solutions, so we could interpret this as an imaginary ellipse.\n\n- Although there are no real solutions, the equation ${x_i^2}=-a^2$ is equivalent to $(x_i - a\\textrm{i})(x_i + a\\textrm{i})=0$ which in turn gives equations of two imaginary parallel lines $x_i =a\\textrm{i}$ and $x_i=-a\\textrm{i}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "81eb37e3-202d-5934-93ce-8534c3ffc1a0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5131 :tutorial/title "Empty Sets"} :step/id #uuid "980cb0f2-0635-5e7b-926e-f8cbd302bf7b" :step/math-academy-id 32211 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172888}}] :activity/title "Classifying Quadratic Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172167}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572775} :activity/id #uuid "6e371bb6-888d-5045-993d-bc517a769511" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050760 :topic/id #uuid "64670a5e-dd35-51fc-bd24-210590f74fcd" :topic/math-academy-id 3336} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572743 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172622 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ec62dfe-79c3-5df9-aac6-8a3a17aaaecd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A pair of parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171358} {:answer/id #uuid "e6399f6c-b04f-5ecf-8053-726d3de3c39d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $3$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171359} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf8347a-dfca-5952-96d3-427ecf3f2518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $9$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171360} {:answer/id #uuid "729cf01c-5ec6-5b07-a19b-839c49047a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171361} {:answer/id #uuid "7d71e306-7fc7-589b-914c-2f234e7856ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171362}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f31f2330-95df-50e5-a4cd-753dc047d887" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7dcde9f5-c70c-5225-9e3c-4744c661314a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95402" :question/problem "What type of a quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n$2x_{1}^{2} + 3x_{2}^{2} - 4x_{1} + 12x_{2} =-8$"} :step/id #uuid "27f2cabb-08ac-5ca7-a849-181634c81b0c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572792}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572775 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366629 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b13d7123-12c3-5fba-865d-79d04d134405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A pair of intersecting lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366631} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb75fec-aad0-52b8-8f0b-256603947924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366632} {:answer/id #uuid "335c53ad-9b06-5ae9-9d82-ab45fdedd159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366633} {:answer/id #uuid "50865aa7-b849-5510-a201-43adab487ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A pair of parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366634} {:answer/id #uuid "30cf17d6-9da7-5a11-b299-70e72069e0e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes  $3$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366635}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366633} :answer-field/id #uuid "23070fd2-4a47-5c26-ae55-52058929b2df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3c25a282-f09e-5080-ae4d-6a92631e6519" :question/math-academy-id "q-95397" :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}=4.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $2{y}_{1}^{2}$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}=4\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1} & =4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}+4\\sqrt{2}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2}) & =4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{1}-4{y}_{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{1}-4{y}_{2} & =4 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{1})-4{y}_{2} & =4 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{1}+1-1)-4{y}_{2} & =4 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}+1)^{2}-2\\cdot 1-4{y}_{2} & =4 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & =4{y}_{2}+6 \\\\ 2({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & =4({y}_{2}+\\frac{3}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1} &={y}_{1}+1, \\\\ {z}_{2} &={y}_{2}+\\frac{3}{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({y}_{1}+1)^{2} & =4({y}_{2}+\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\ 2{z}_{1}^{2} & =4{z}_{2} \\\\ {z}_{1}^{2} & =2{z}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${z}_{2}$-axis."} :step/id #uuid "81a51d56-4664-5776-ac0c-6fd45390f9dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572801}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572792 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "259e4eb3-4b40-5254-9306-2cdbae4d082f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A single point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404866} {:answer/id #uuid "84eb9ae9-484d-5bb3-abcb-0e5d6c244735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404867} {:answer/id #uuid "a8704e70-86f6-5b66-8725-e7aa791842f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404868} {:answer/id #uuid "374654e9-d95a-5470-b619-3f7c557f6027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A pair of parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404869} {:answer/id #uuid "0f04bc86-c555-5e60-95dd-3ee129807d0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404870}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404866} :answer-field/id #uuid "f725653a-5c15-5cd9-8b78-52c824803bf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ca23a2a3-705b-526c-8692-0652a1e980bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-95998" :question/problem "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{17}{4}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the squares for the variables ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & =-\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ ({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1})+({x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{2}) & =-\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ ({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2})+({x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}) & =-\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ ({x}_{1}+2)^{2}-{2}^{2}+{({x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =-\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ ({x}_{1}+2)^{2}-4+{({x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4} & =-\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ ({x}_{1}+2)^{2}+{({x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nA sum of squares is equal to zero if and only if each summand is zero, so the given quadratic equation represents the single point $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane."} :step/id #uuid "bdb406eb-4bde-5255-b0ae-21eca3b0a923" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572808}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572801 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572671 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52c08f07-4189-509a-b220-5216f042453d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572415} {:answer/id #uuid "8635fe6e-fac6-55c1-996c-c85b1599c832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An circle of radius $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572461} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d80198-d0f9-5a4a-bda4-ee01da631e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572491} {:answer/id #uuid "a5629f9b-9362-5ae4-8c5b-612ef5f66982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A pair of intersecting lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572493} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3f94a7-6795-5611-8617-8bdcc199a8ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572546}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572493} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1a95532-8eb9-58d2-950f-597816548f39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "13426e81-09a1-5f21-879b-4654e3684393" :question/math-academy-id "q-95398" :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=2\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2})-2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2}) & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2} & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}) & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}-(\\sqrt{2})^{2}) & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}+2-2) & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}+\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+2 & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}+\\sqrt{2})}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1} &={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2} &={y}_{2}+\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}_{1}^{2}-{z}_{2}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the pair of intersecting lines ${z}_{2}=\\sqrt{3}{z}_{1}$ and ${z}_{2}=-\\sqrt{3}{z}_{1}.$"} :step/id #uuid "e8039f07-e5ec-5025-bed0-a9260a404806" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572743}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572808 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366732 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "050d3072-c838-5e08-b0bc-4d5171d8a819" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A single point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366734} {:answer/id #uuid "e5183501-b9cd-5561-b6e3-c0b1b0bb5462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366735} {:answer/id #uuid "c04e7a5e-a95d-5547-bc90-7be6e5c15598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366736} {:answer/id #uuid "3ed40f62-9010-5b5c-bf46-6927e9dc6923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A pair of parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366737} {:answer/id #uuid "4d718142-0fc1-5c0e-bd69-6c41ab31ffc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366738}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366738} :answer-field/id #uuid "296453d6-3c71-5737-82c3-c5c36630c17c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bb83c793-f479-5cb9-b03e-05eb6c2cfac2" :question/math-academy-id "q-96075" :question/problem "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=\\frac{35}{3}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the squares for the variable ${x}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{35}{3} \\\\ 3({x}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1})+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{35}{3} \\\\ 3({x}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1}+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2})+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{35}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-3{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{35}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{35}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{36}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &={x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ {y}_{2} &={x}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents an ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "fd94ea8f-f9bc-5902-a020-afbc76230738"}] :activity/title "Classifying Quadratic Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572306}]}
